# Testing

## Where tests go

- Unit tests sit next to the code in `#[cfg(test)] mod tests`.
- Behavior across modules goes in the crate's `tests/`.
- Fuzz targets go in the crate's `fuzz/`. They use cargo-fuzz, need nightly, and
  stay out of the workspace.
- Benchmarks for hot paths (decode, encode, color conversion) go in `benches/`.

## Rules

- Tests are deterministic. No network, no sleeps, and no dependence on test order.
- Timing belongs in benchmarks, never in test asserts.
- A test checks one behavior, and its name says which:
  `rejects_sps_with_zero_width`, not `test_sps_2`.
- Every bug fix comes with a test that failed before the fix.
- A test that needs hardware the machine lacks skips with a printed reason. It
  never passes by silently doing nothing.

## Media fixtures

- Keep committed fixtures small, under 1 MB each. Larger conformance suites are
  downloaded by a script that checks each file's SHA-256.
- Shared fixtures live in `fixtures/` at the repo root, and
  `crates/testing/fixtures` loads them. Note the source and license of every
  fixture in `fixtures/README.md`. Only commit files we may redistribute.
- Reference checksums come from the codec's reference decoder (JM, HM, libvpx,
  libaom, and so on). Record the tool and version next to the checksums.
- FFmpeg may generate reference data offline. Tests never call FFmpeg or any
  other external tool.

## Fuzzing

Every software decoder and container parser has a fuzz target. Each crash it
finds becomes a regression test in `tests/`.
